==Latin==

===Etymology===
Uncertain. Perhaps form {{proto|Indo-European|kaput-|lang=la}} with [[cognate|cognates]] including {{etyl|de|-}} {{term|Haupt|lang=de}} and English {{term|head|lang=en}} (< Germanic ''*haubidaz''), however phonetics remain problematic.

===Noun===
{{la-noun|caput|capitis|capitis|n|third}}

# [[head]]
# [[anatomical]] term; headlike [[protuberance]] on an [[organ]] or [[body]] part, usually [[bone]], for instance ''caput [[ulna|ulnae]]''
# a [[disease]]; a severe swelling of the soft tissues of a newborn's scalp that develops as the baby travels through the birth canal.
# {{figuratively}} The [[vital]] part
# {{context|of a river}} [[origin]], [[source]], [[head]]
#* [[w:en:Quintus Horatus Flaccus|Horace]], ''Carmina'', 1:1
#*: ''nunc ad aquae lene '''caput''' sacrae.''
# {{figuratively}} [[life]]
# [[capital city]]
#* [[w:Livy|Titus Livius]], ''Ab Urbe Condita'', 1:16
#*: ''ut mea Roma '''caput''' orbis terrarum sit''
#* [[w:en:Ovid|Ovid]], ''Ab Urbe Condita'', 8:4
#*: ''Romam '''caput''' Latio esse''
# {{context|poetic|lang=la}} [[leader]], [[chief]]
# [[chapter]]
# {{inflection of|caput|caput|acc|s}}
# {{inflection of|caput|caput|voc|s}}

====Usage notes====
''Caput'' can be used with either a genitive or a dative in the sense of a [[capital city]].

